Dear Hiring Team,

It is with great pleasure that I submit my candidacy for the *Software Development Engineer* role on the *OpenSearch Control Plane* team at *Amazon Web Services*. I bring over three years of professional software development experience building and operating large-scale distributed backend systems in Java, Python, and TypeScript, along with a strong academic foundation as a Master of Science in Computer Science graduate from Pace University, NY.

Across two engineering roles I have designed, shipped, and operated the kind of distributed control-plane systems this position demands. As a Software Engineer at Sperse, I designed and operate a distributed orchestration control plane in Python and Temporal that automates the full lifecycle of long-running workflows — provisioning, scale-out, retry, and fail-over — using idempotent activities and blast-radius-scoped failure domains that hold high availability and static stability through partial node failure. On top of it I built a multi-tenant CRM platform with LangGraph and FastAPI serving 10,000+ users, routing requests across 40+ sub-agents with tenant isolation and JWT-scoped authorization designed around real failure modes rather than bolted on afterward. I also engineered a production MCP server with FastMCP exposing 160+ backend actions, where the highest-leverage work was applying Object Oriented Design and design patterns to consolidate duplicate abstractions into a single validated tool layer with retries and metrics — exactly the "simplify, remove what doesn't earn its keep" instinct your team describes. Earlier, at Qualitest, I designed a cloud-agnostic Storage Service in Java and Spring Boot with PostgreSQL and Redis serving uploads, downloads, and deletes across Google Cloud Storage, S3, and MinIO with durability and fail-over across backends, and orchestrated large-scale long-running jobs with AWS Step Functions that cut AWS Lambda timeouts and hardened error recovery through bounded retries and dead-letter replay across 100K+ weekly executions. I also tuned PostgreSQL with window functions, materialized views, and composite indexes to reduce query latency and improve scaling of a multi-tiered analytics tier for cross-functional stakeholders.

I read the line "operations is core engineering on this team, not overhead" as the most important sentence in the posting, because it matches how I already work. I own operations end-to-end for the services I build: OpenTelemetry traces, structured log analytics, and latency/error dashboards drive my incident response and RCAs, and I convert recurring pages into automated operational tooling rather than absorbing them as toil. At Qualitest I carried the same discipline through production on-call, owning CI/CD, code reviews, source control management, and build processes, and driving RCAs that removed dead code and configuration sprawl from the deployment path. I apply AI coding assistants and GenAI tools — Claude Code and OpenAI Codex — across the SDLC from design and testing through code review and debugging, triaging 25-30 PRs per week while validating every generated change for correctness and security before it merges. Outside of work, I built Dark Factory, a distributed orchestrator that decomposes large requests into dependency-aware task DAGs scheduled across a fleet of multi-machine agents, with heartbeat-based node failure detection that reschedules a crashed node in seconds without losing progress — the same failure-detection and recovery problem the OpenSearch control plane solves at a far larger scale.

I have always been drawn to Amazon's commitment to customer obsession and operational excellence — starting from the customer and working backward, and treating reliability as a design constraint instead of a phase after launch — and I would be glad to bring that mindset to a service that hundreds of thousands of customers depend on for mission-critical search, observability, and AI-powered retrieval. I would love to discuss my interest in this position with your team and can be reached at #ph or #eml. Thank you for your time and consideration, I look forward to connecting!

